WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.360 --> 00:01:08.800
Tim Ferriss: Soman, good sir.
Soman Chainani: Thanks for having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.360
me here. This is going to be fun.
Tim Ferriss: So nice to see you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.040
And it's hard to believe this is, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.000
our first episode in person.
Soman Chainani: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.440
which I think makes it more real, somehow.
Tim Ferriss: Makes it more real. Definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.240
makes it more colorful. But before we&nbsp;
get to the colorful, Soman Chainani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.480
for those who don't know, who are you?
Soman Chainani: I think of myself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:36.000
specialist in the teenage mind, and I access&nbsp;
that by being an author of young adult fantasy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.200
using everything I can about connecting to young&nbsp;
people and lovers of fantasy through novels. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.920
that's what I've been doing for the last 10 years.
Tim Ferriss: And for people who might want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.880
dig into that further, any works&nbsp;
you might suggest they start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.120
or check out, website, anything like that?
Soman Chainani: I wrote a series called The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.360
School for Good and Evil for 10 years. There's six&nbsp;
books in the series, and Netflix made a movie out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.760
of it that came out last year. And then another&nbsp;
book a little bit for older audiences is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:13.583
Beats and Beauty, which is going to be a TV show&nbsp;
sometime soon. So those are my two main things.

00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:18.080
Tim Ferriss: And we'll link to everything in&nbsp;
the show notes for folks. I have explored your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:26.080
writing, and we're looking at some of your&nbsp;
writing, your handwriting on cards of various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:35.040
colors. We have orange neatly lined up in one&nbsp;
row, yellow, and then purple. And this alludes to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:42.080
in a sense, your beautiful pursuit of symmetry&nbsp;
and color and also the format of the show.&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.760
So I had sent you a note about this fun&nbsp;
experimental format, which is five things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.360
you're excited about, five things you've changed&nbsp;
your mind about, and then five things that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.920
absurd that you still do, or do, and tweak that&nbsp;
a little bit, and we'll see if we get to all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.400
them. The point is really just to provide a launch&nbsp;
pad for conversation, but I was thinking in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:10.600
of naming, Five of Three is not terribly catchy,&nbsp;
but Four by Four, like the piece of wood, is kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:17.951
of catchy. So we could do four of each of these&nbsp;
and then add four people you are interested in —&nbsp;

00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:19.600
Soman Chainani: It works for me.
Tim Ferriss: — or following, or who people should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.960
know more about. And you so delightfully put out&nbsp;
these cards and offered me dealer's choice. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:33.360
there are many things we could go to here.
Give people some foreshadowing. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.280
"Intermittent pop star," "Cross-collar dating,"&nbsp;
"Your bull might be gay," "NYC L.A.," but we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.960
not going to start there. We are going to&nbsp;
start with "Follow the flow," speaking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.600
catchy. So what does "Follow the flow" mean?
Soman Chainani: It's interesting because every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.360
time I've decided to write a novel, like what&nbsp;
my next book is going to be, and I telegraph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.520
this to my agent and my publisher for a couple&nbsp;
of years. When it comes time to write that book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.440
I'll start it, but it never ends up being the&nbsp;
book I write next, ever. And what I realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:17.680
is that your conscious mind can't actually solve&nbsp;
problems. It can execute, it can deliver on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.560
promise of something, but what actually is going&nbsp;
to be the next creative force in your life, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.560
next big decision, has to come to you naturally.
So it's this idea of the decision coming to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:33.480
rather than you making it. And I think, especially&nbsp;
in creative work, where it's so important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.920
Obviously I'm going to be working two or three&nbsp;
years on a novel. My brain will not let me work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:46.760
something unless it decides it. So I've realized&nbsp;
more and more as time goes on that I am just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:53.600
manager. I'm the manager of the creativity. I'm&nbsp;
not the actual creator. The elves are the creator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.320
They're going to tell me what to do. They're going&nbsp;
to be the ones who tell you what the next book's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:00.320
going to be. They're the ones who are going to&nbsp;
come up with the chapter titles. They're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.160
to do everything. I just have to show up.
Tim Ferriss: I have so many follow-ups.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.667
May I dive in?
Soman Chainani: Please.&nbsp;

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:10.384
Tim Ferriss: All right. So&nbsp;
I aspire to embrace this.&nbsp;

00:05:10.384 --> 00:05:18.280
Soman Chainani: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: I am like an over-caffeinated Swiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:27.480
manufacturing plant manager with OCD and various&nbsp;
other monkish tendencies. So I think the manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.143
is kind of running the asylum, so to speak —
Soman Chainani: Yes, yes, yes.&nbsp;

00:05:30.143 --> 00:05:33.720
Tim Ferriss: — on my side. And that doesn't&nbsp;
mean I don't have moments of inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:40.960
but I justify this by having a schedule, [foreign&nbsp;
language], and I kick out a certain amount over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.320
certain period of time, whatever it might be. That&nbsp;
is a superpower that is also a super weakness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:52.320
in excess. Now on the opposite side of the&nbsp;
spectrum, I saw this post on social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.720
be careful, folks. It's a jungle out there, but I&nbsp;
did see a post that I thought was kind of funny.&nbsp;

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.880
And we're sitting here in Austin, there's a lot of&nbsp;
conspirituality here, a lot of aspiring shamanist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:08.800
Earth goddesses running amok, and their male&nbsp;
equivalents. And this post on social said, "A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.320
woman who lives in the moment finds out that that&nbsp;
moment is connected to all other moments." And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:21.080
was like this sad, fake Onion article about not&nbsp;
being able to plan a life. You clearly ship in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:28.520
sense that you publish books, you are very engaged&nbsp;
with the world. You're not just a ship without a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:35.800
sail, without a rudder, getting blown everywhere,&nbsp;
to and fro. So how do you precipitate, or think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.587
about inviting, the muse? Does that make sense?
Soman Chainani: Yeah, 100 percent.&nbsp;

00:06:39.587 --> 00:06:43.411
Tim Ferriss: Because you could&nbsp;
just be waiting for 10, 20 years.&nbsp;

00:06:43.411 --> 00:06:49.040
Soman Chainani: 100 percent. I think what ends up&nbsp;
happening is that I know what my conscious mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:57.160
can do. So for instance, every publisher wants me&nbsp;
to write a fairy tale fantasy next, because School&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:02.240
for Good and Evil was my first. It was abnormally&nbsp;
successful. Therefore, I will get paid infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.720
amount of money, have infinite amount of resources&nbsp;
to write a fairy tale fantasy. I had a fairy tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.360
fantasy that I wanted to do. I wanted to reinvent&nbsp;
Neverland, sort of do a Game of Thrones version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.520
of Neverland. I had the whole thing planned out.&nbsp;
My conscious brain mapped out, over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.440
of a year and a half, an entire world while I&nbsp;
was writing School for Good and Evil. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.600
doodle it in notebooks. I had everything.&nbsp;
And then when it came time to write it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:31.600
I wrote the first two chapters, went to sleep, and&nbsp;
the next day was like, "This is not my next book."&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:38.280
And it came from just this feeling of which the&nbsp;
only way I can describe it is there was part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:45.080
me that is like, "We are not going to spend&nbsp;
two years following the wrong impulse," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:53.920
the wrong impulse was money, easy route, the&nbsp;
easy route. And instead, this other idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.880
I've been noodling around and been trying to&nbsp;
give away to people for the last five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.960
I had told everyone this idea being like, "Someone&nbsp;
should do this idea. This idea is super important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:12.680
Someone should do this. It's not me. I can't do&nbsp;
this. This is too hard." I just woke up and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:17.680
like, "Okay, you know what? That idea, let's try&nbsp;
writing it as a short story instead because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.200
idea is so important, and no one's done it, and&nbsp;
everyone says it can't be done or whatever." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.000
I start working on it, give it to my agent.
Tim Ferriss: Can you mention the idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.880
or is it still under wraps?
Soman Chainani: Not yet. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:34.040
because we're about to take it out. But meanwhile,&nbsp;
my poor agent, he knows that whatever I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.840
the next thing's going to be, "It's not&nbsp;
going to be the next thing." So I tell him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.400
"I'm just going to try this idea as a&nbsp;
short story," because we've sold short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.960
stories before. He's like, "Okay." While&nbsp;
he comes up with his next fairy tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.560
he will do his short story. Do the short story.
Tim Ferriss: Must appease the talent. Fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.680
I'll get you your green M&amp;Ms.
Soman Chainani: He's just waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:58.080
for me to come back with Neverland, Wonderland,&nbsp;
he doesn't care what it is, happily ever after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:05.240
princess, princesses. So I start working on&nbsp;
this short story, and I start getting into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.560
and then I feel that feeling. It's what&nbsp;
I felt with School for Good and Evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:15.520
which is I don't care who reads this, I don't&nbsp;
care what happens to this. This is mine. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:22.107
one's taking it away from me. I am doing this at&nbsp;
the expense of all other things. I don't care.&nbsp;

00:09:22.107 --> 00:09:28.840
Tim Ferriss: What are the symptoms of that?&nbsp;
Is it that you wake up earlier than normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:32.640
and that's the first thing you want to&nbsp;
do? Is it that — I'm making things up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:38.360
of course. Is it a giddy feeling that just&nbsp;
doesn't occur normally in other circumstances?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.520
What is the signature of that feeling?
Soman Chainani: It's more like you feel like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.984
cult leader. You're like, "This is the thing."
Tim Ferriss: This is the true way.&nbsp;

00:09:45.984 --> 00:09:51.840
Soman Chainani: Yeah, this is now my church.&nbsp;
People are going to join. I just need to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:57.240
the thing. It becomes your whole personality.
Tim Ferriss: So is it fair to say it's a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.864
of conviction and confidence that&nbsp;
you couldn't analytically justify?&nbsp;

00:10:00.864 --> 00:10:04.280
Soman Chainani: No, you can't change — in&nbsp;
fact, it's funny. We took it to my current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:09.640
publisher, not even 10 pages of it and just the&nbsp;
general thing of the ideas. And I've been with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:16.280
them for 10 years writing fairy tales, and they&nbsp;
were like, the idea of me moving into a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:22.240
age bracket, and this is not fairy tales at&nbsp;
all. This is far from it as possible. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:28.560
wouldn't even entertain the idea, wouldn't even&nbsp;
go there. And it still did nothing to shake me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:35.360
whatsoever. I was just like, "Oh," and it's funny&nbsp;
because my parents, my agent, everyone's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:44.440
"What are you..." Clearly your second thing should&nbsp;
be to capitalize on that, and nothing in my body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:50.720
will let me do it. So I have been happy as a clam&nbsp;
working on this other thing, and I realized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:11:00.320
it comes down to what you want your life to be.&nbsp;
And mine is not getting diminishing returns on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:05.920
ghost of a past work, even for a lot of money.
Tim Ferriss: I remember, I believe it was BJ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.720
Novak, who's written a million things,&nbsp;
very talented, very funny comedian also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:18.760
said whenever he says to himself, "But the money&nbsp;
is so good," that's a flag. And I'm paraphrasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:23.880
here, but in this particular case, so I'm&nbsp;
going to prod a little bit, you can refuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.547
but is it fiction? Nonfiction?
Soman Chainani: It's fiction.&nbsp;

00:11:26.547 --> 00:11:28.640
Tim Ferriss: It's fiction, okay.
Soman Chainani: It's a fiction that I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:36.240
like to happen. So it's a predictive fiction.&nbsp;
And it's so funny because when we announce it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.504
which will be soon enough, all these things that&nbsp;
we're going to talk about today, it's all there.&nbsp;

00:11:41.504 --> 00:11:44.984
Tim Ferriss: So it's like The Usual Suspects&nbsp;
with "Kobayashi" on the cup. Wait a minute.&nbsp;

00:11:44.984 --> 00:11:48.627
Soman Chainani: Everything, it's all&nbsp;
going to make sense when we're done.&nbsp;

00:11:48.627 --> 00:11:51.680
Tim Ferriss: I cannot wait to see how&nbsp;
"Your bull might be gay" fits into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.680 --> 00:12:00.480
this. All right, so it seems like "Follow the&nbsp;
flow" has been checked. Now, that seems to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:06.440
this is fun. I might have people do this, although&nbsp;
I'm sure a lot will not be as enthused as — you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:15.040
have very nice handwriting, too. So we've pulled&nbsp;
one of the orange cards. Does this purple "Give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.423
stories away" tie back —
Soman Chainani: It does.&nbsp;

00:12:17.423 --> 00:12:19.720
Tim Ferriss: — to what you were saying?
Soman Chainani: It's the idea of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:26.360
I get a lot of big ideas, and I will not write a&nbsp;
book until I've had the idea for at least three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:31.720
years. It has to stay with me for three years&nbsp;
because it's going to take me that long to write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:38.520
promote, take it out, and I need to be&nbsp;
sure that it's not going to suddenly be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:45.120
short-term relationship. And it feels also like&nbsp;
it's timeless then. If it lasts three years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:49.120
it's made it through most of a presidential&nbsp;
administration. It's made it through all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.320
ups and downs. I feel like it's going to last.
And so first thing I do when I get a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.600
idea that I'm really excited about is I&nbsp;
start to tell everybody. I just am like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:01.680
"Someone should do this." I tell all my&nbsp;
writer friends, I tell anybody. I'm just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.000
like, oh, sometimes I'll just have meetings&nbsp;
with Hollywood companies about, I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.320
adapting other work or stuff like that. I'm&nbsp;
doing it really to just be like, "Someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.520
should do this idea," and sometimes it gets&nbsp;
picked up, and they'll give it to someone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:19.720
or there's many times where I've had ideas or&nbsp;
something and someone has done that version of it.&nbsp;

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:24.640
But if no one takes it, then&nbsp;
after a few years, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:31.760
it's so strong that that's when I do it, so —
Tim Ferriss: I do know of one other person in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.120
my life, friend named Kevin Kelly, I don't know&nbsp;
if you know this name, older gent, has an amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:43.680
Amish-style beard, despite the fact that he is an&nbsp;
incredibly accurate futurist. He's more than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:49.480
but he's very good at predicting trends and&nbsp;
upcoming development. He tries to give away all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:55.280
his ideas, and I don't want to speak for him, but&nbsp;
in a sense, it's in the hopes that other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:59.960
will do the work of implementing those ideas.
Soman Chainani: It's totally selfish. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:06.280
a generous thing at all. It's, "This would&nbsp;
be great. Do it for my entertainment so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:11.920
can see it realized," because there's only&nbsp;
so many things I'm going to be able to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:18.360
I am jealous of songwriters who, if they have&nbsp;
a great idea, they make their one song. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.840
know what I mean? This is, every time I&nbsp;
have a big idea, it's a couple of years.&nbsp;

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:27.400
Tim Ferriss: Now this might seem like a silly&nbsp;
question, but how do you distinguish between ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:33.240
that people won't pick up because they're bad&nbsp;
ideas versus ideas people won't pick up because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.480
for whatever reason, you are uniquely suited to&nbsp;
looking through the prism and seeing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:40.840
they don't see? You get what I mean? If you were&nbsp;
like, "I'm going to start a waste management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:46.600
company in Tuscaloosa," and people are like,&nbsp;
"I'm not going to do that," that is fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:51.960
different from what you're describing.
Soman Chainani: Yeah, I always say the test is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:57.520
can someone else do it? So that's when I have the&nbsp;
idea. I'm always like, "Sure, someone else can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:03.800
this." But then, if they don't and time goes on,&nbsp;
and this is what happened with this idea because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:08.320
when I first had it a few years ago, I thought,&nbsp;
"I don't know how to do that. It's too hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.600
It's just not in my wheelhouse." And as the years&nbsp;
went by, little by little, I started to be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:16.480
"Well, what if I could do this? And this plays&nbsp;
to my strengths, and move this around. I start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.440
to see the..." Then all of a sudden, you realize&nbsp;
you're ready. You know what I mean? So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:27.080
it's about can you do it? Like School for Good&nbsp;
and Evil came out at a time where that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:31.360
there were eight other books about magic schools&nbsp;
because it had been 10 years since Harry Potter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:34.440
and it felt like 10 years later, the&nbsp;
floodgates opened and everyone was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:40.240
now all the narcissists who think they can compete&nbsp;
with J.K. Rowling are ready to show their wares.&nbsp;

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:47.600
And agent was frantic, the publisher was nervous,&nbsp;
everyone was nervous, because there were much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:54.000
