from his work, but I would be like, "Yeah, no."
Soman Chainani: No.&nbsp;

00:37:53.499 --> 00:37:56.720
Tim Ferriss: No way. Maybe he's doing&nbsp;
jeans commercials, maybe he's doing car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:02.680
commercials. But his artwork is incredible. Okay,&nbsp;
we will definitely link to that. Check it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:08.800
folks @christophermarleystudio. All right,&nbsp;
check, check. My next follow on Instagram.&nbsp;

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.440
see. I thought it was conch and then I was&nbsp;
like, "Oh, wait a second. That's an A."&nbsp;

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:20.424
Soman Chainani: Oh, that's an A. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Coach Alpha.&nbsp;

00:38:20.424 --> 00:38:23.984
Soman Chainani: Coach Alpha.
Tim Ferriss: What or who is Coach Alpha?&nbsp;

00:38:23.984 --> 00:38:27.600
Soman Chainani: Okay. I realized that I can&nbsp;
only do two jobs in my life. There's only two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:33.560
things I'm good at and there's only one thing&nbsp;
I would like to do as much as I like writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:40.840
and that is I feel like — and it's a&nbsp;
job that should exist, and it doesn't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:45.544
Generation Alpha, which is teenagers of today —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I didn't know that was a thing.&nbsp;

00:38:45.544 --> 00:38:47.424
Soman Chainani: Yeah. So after —
Tim Ferriss: We've gone back to A.&nbsp;

00:38:47.424 --> 00:38:51.840
Soman Chainani: Yes, so after Gen Z is Generation&nbsp;
Alpha, and that's like who are teenagers now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:57.440
They're growing up in an impossible situation&nbsp;
with everything that's happening with the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.440 --> 00:39:02.760
and the phones getting more addict — it's just&nbsp;
impossible. And when I was a tutor, I was a tutor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:06.480
for 10 years, and what I loved the most was when&nbsp;
— because I could tutor every subject. I was such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:10.240
a nerd in school that I just really could tutor&nbsp;
any subject. And I loved it when a parent just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:19.880
outsourced their entire child's home life to me.
But I loved it because I could get to the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:27.960
of what made them tick and what was important, and&nbsp;
starting to really not just help them with school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:33.480
but get them on a better track, like out of their&nbsp;
phone, and stop worrying about what everybody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:37.840
wanted them to be, including their parents,&nbsp;
and more focusing on who they actually were.&nbsp;

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:43.720
The mental health crisis that's affecting almost&nbsp;
all teenagers is this sort of existential dread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:48.600
And so I would've loved to, if I hadn't become&nbsp;
a writer, and I still feel like this is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:55.240
to be part of my life somehow, is I would love&nbsp;
to be the Mary Poppins who can go into a house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.240 --> 00:40:00.480
and just be like, "Let's get this on track." I'll&nbsp;
give you an example in a way that parents can't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:07.240
One kid I met was getting off track because he was&nbsp;
drinking a lot and getting in trouble and all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:12.600
sort of stuff. And I noticed that every time I&nbsp;
saw him, he was talking about going out, partying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:16.320
and at the end of the night having wrestling&nbsp;
matches with all his friends and just getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:20.240
into these physical fights with people. And he&nbsp;
was always beating up on his younger brothers.&nbsp;

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:26.400
Every time I saw him they were just always&nbsp;
physical with this thing. And one day we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:28.824
trying to solve all the other stuff —
Tim Ferriss: The academic stuff.&nbsp;

00:40:28.824 --> 00:40:32.320
Soman Chainani: Yeah, just everything else.&nbsp;
And I thought to myself — actually I said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:38.000
him. I said — and his whole goal in life was to be&nbsp;
popular and party and all that stuff. And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:44.360
"I don't know if partying is your goal in reality.&nbsp;
I feel like your real goal is that you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:51.800
fight. That's what you actually want to do." What&nbsp;
you really want to do is not drink and hang out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:55.040
with your friends. It's you want to drink so you&nbsp;
can beat them up at the end of the night and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.040 --> 00:41:03.600
into these aggro — every night ended in aggro.
And I researched. I said, "There's a UFC gym like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:12.000
four minutes from your house. Go take a class."&nbsp;
16 year old, go take a class. Takes a class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:18.240
loves it. Is now there three, four times a week,&nbsp;
having the time of his life. Drinking went way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:24.800
down. Everything all got back on track. Got a&nbsp;
job to fund the UFC thing thing. Everything's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:29.027
fine. No more beating up the brothers. He&nbsp;
didn't need to. He can beat up anyone —&nbsp;

00:41:29.027 --> 00:41:31.120
Tim Ferriss: Just needed an outlet.
Soman Chainani: Just needed an outlet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:35.680
But what parent's going to say, "Go fight&nbsp;
after school." It's just, they're not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.680 --> 00:41:42.560
to do it. And so it's stuff like that where I&nbsp;
just feel like sometimes kids need to be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:49.200
for who they are. And in order for us to get&nbsp;
out of this crisis, I think people are like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:54.520
"Oh, mental health crisis, all these things."&nbsp;
I feel like there needs to be a third party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.120 --> 00:42:01.320
whether it's even as much as teen groups. I was&nbsp;
just thinking about the fact that on any given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:06.080
night, if you're an alcoholic or you have a drug&nbsp;
addiction or whatever, you can just go to an AA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:11.640
meeting and you can have that group experience.
And I felt like, why isn't there that for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:15.760
teenagers? This idea that you can just go to&nbsp;
a space and meet other teenagers on a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:20.200
night and have that kind of place to talk to or&nbsp;
experience. And yes, you can do it online. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:24.200
just think the more you get into your phone,&nbsp;
the further you often get away from actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:31.120
who you are and real connection. So I don't&nbsp;
know. I have no formal thoughts on how this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:37.267
should happen. I do miss that part of my old job,&nbsp;
which was going into the house and helping kids.&nbsp;

00:42:37.267 --> 00:42:40.600
Tim Ferriss: Footnote for people who may&nbsp;
have missed our earlier conversations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:45.440
well into your writing career, correct me if&nbsp;
I'm wrong, fact check, please, you held onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:54.520
your tutoring because it gave you a safety net&nbsp;
but also, I guess, the psychological reassurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.520 --> 00:43:00.120
of knowing that you didn't have to white-knuckle,&nbsp;
desperately make the first buck that came your way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:03.000
through the writing. Is that a fair description?&nbsp;
We don't have to spend too much time on it.&nbsp;

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:04.800
Soman Chainani: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: But it's important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:11.880
as an example to me because a lot of creatives who&nbsp;
do really well do not burn the ships behind them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:21.904
They do not block all the exits. They actually&nbsp;
have some type of backup in the form that you did.&nbsp;

00:43:21.904 --> 00:43:26.440
Soman Chainani: 100 percent, because also it meant&nbsp;
that I could take all the risks in my writing. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:32.800
even now I know I can do other jobs. I could go&nbsp;
back to tutoring, I could teach at a university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:39.360
I could work with kids and teenagers in some&nbsp;
way. So I'm happy to take the risk on this book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:45.240
instead of chasing the money because there's no&nbsp;
pressure on it. So I think that's the important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:51.680
thing. I feel like if you're writing with that&nbsp;
desperate white-knuckle, mercenary for money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:55.880
it increases the chance that what you're&nbsp;
writing is, you're trying to fit a mold.&nbsp;

00:43:55.880 --> 00:44:03.000
Tim Ferriss: So Coach Alpha, is this a public&nbsp;
attempt to give away an idea? Let's just say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.987
you can't get a taker.
Soman Chainani: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:44:04.987 --> 00:44:08.360
Tim Ferriss: Let's just say you don't see it.&nbsp;
Would you ever think of doing — I know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:14.840
isn't in person, but would you ever think of&nbsp;
doing a series of talks and then pulling from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:14.840 --> 00:44:19.360
that and putting together a course? Making&nbsp;
lectures available for free? Maybe they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:26.000
targeted parents, maybe they're targeted kids.&nbsp;
If this for whatever reason doesn't get takers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:30.407
this Coach Alpha, because it also requires — it's&nbsp;
not just an idea that can be executed by anyone.&nbsp;

00:44:30.407 --> 00:44:31.840
Soman Chainani: Yep.
Tim Ferriss: But to underscore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:38.600
something you said, the third-party piece is&nbsp;
really important. I can get not anyone but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:44.240
almost anyone to change behaviors sometimes except&nbsp;
for the people closest to me because, to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:52.600
I'm just Tim, and sometimes you can develop&nbsp;
a selective deafness around the people you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:59.064
closest to. So then when you have a third party&nbsp;
who's credible, there's a lot of leverage there.&nbsp;

00:44:59.064 --> 00:45:02.800
Soman Chainani: Well, even I was thinking about&nbsp;
teenagers. Let's say you have — and I've told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:07.440
parents this many times, and most of the time&nbsp;
they didn't listen, but I gave them the secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:12.360
which was, when your kid takes initiative and&nbsp;
does something on their own, like they start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:21.960
making their bed or they start working out&nbsp;
after school, do not mention it. Don't say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:26.440
"It's great you made the bed," or, "That's so cool&nbsp;
that you're working out." Do not. Then it's your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:30.920
thing. That means every time they're going to&nbsp;
go make the bed, they're now thinking of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:35.800
approval and, little by little, it's going to&nbsp;
stop them doing it because it's now become your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:40.080
thing. Leave it. It's their thing.
Tim Ferriss: How many kids have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:44.585
you tutored over the years, would you say?
Soman Chainani: Oh, God. At least a few hundred.&nbsp;

00:45:44.585 --> 00:45:45.920
Tim Ferriss: A few hundred?
Soman Chainani: Yeah, probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:52.520
a few hundred. Well, I miss it, but also I got&nbsp;
the connection through the books. And also what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.520 --> 00:45:57.040
funny is I started doing writing camps where&nbsp;
I got hosted by writing camps. And there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.040 --> 00:46:01.920
always a curriculum that they wanted us to teach&nbsp;
or whatever, and I would just go in there and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:07.320
my own thing. And the first thing I would work on&nbsp;
with each kid is, when you write, you need to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:13.400
what your characters want, and you need to know at&nbsp;
the deepest basis level. So let's say a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:18.760
wants to play lacrosse. He wants to be a champion&nbsp;
lacrosse player. That's where most people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:24.200
stop. But you need to ask, "Well, why does he want&nbsp;
to be a champion lacrosse player? In order to…"&nbsp;

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:26.720
Tim Ferriss: Why does he want to&nbsp;
drink? That's not enough. It's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:28.360
he wants to fight at the end of the night.
Soman Chainani: Wants to fight. And why does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:31.880
he want to fight at the end of the day? You have&nbsp;
to keep going down until you hit a base level. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.880 --> 00:46:36.600
he trying to save his self-esteem? Is he trying&nbsp;
to prove something to his family? Whatever it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.600 --> 00:46:40.960
And then on the second day, after we work on that&nbsp;
with characters, I do that for themselves. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:45.320
teenager, I'm like, "What do you want?" In front&nbsp;
of the class, so there's usually 12 people. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.280
that's when you start to get somewhere.
Tim Ferriss: Man, I feel like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:51.000
could use that exercise now.
Soman Chainani: But that's how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:56.080
for college essays — my specialty when I was&nbsp;
tutoring was college essays. I used to go in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.080 --> 00:47:00.880
there and help them come up with the perfect&nbsp;
essay topic. And all I needed was three days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:05.360
with them. And I would spend three days asking&nbsp;
that question over and over, and looking at pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:09.880
of their life and us talking about it until we&nbsp;
came up with, what do you want and why do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:15.920
want it? And then making the essay from there.
Tim Ferriss: All right, I'll bite. So what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:23.160
next step? Once you go through that exercise,&nbsp;
how do you craft the narrative, the essay? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:30.280
do you put it into essay form? Is it describing&nbsp;
really what you want? Is that the topic of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:34.720
essay or do you use that as grist for the mill?
Soman Chainani: It becomes the heart of it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:37.787
then from there you can start to build everything&nbsp;
out. I'm thinking about an essay where —&nbsp;

00:47:37.787 --> 00:47:39.880
Tim Ferriss: Did you start doing&nbsp;
this before you were working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:39.880 --> 00:47:43.400
fiction or did it happen simultaneously?
Soman Chainani: Simultaneously. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:46.227
as I was working on School for&nbsp;
Good and Evil the thing I had —&nbsp;

00:47:46.227 --> 00:47:47.880
Tim Ferriss: You had to think about it.
Soman Chainani: And also because I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:50.960
come from film school where they drill into&nbsp;
you, what does your character want? Why do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:55.040
they want it? And I did that every page of that&nbsp;
book, which is why I think it reads quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:58.520
because you just know what everybody wants&nbsp;
at any given moment. And I realized that I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.520 --> 00:48:03.160
working with these kids and no one knows what&nbsp;
they want. They follow the flow in the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:10.120
direction because the flow is taking them towards&nbsp;
anxiety and comparison and all the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:16.480
the world — if I spent my life on Instagram and&nbsp;
TikTok, I would die because it's just the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:25.120
inputs to make me find what I want to do.
Tim Ferriss: Coach Alpha. I love it. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:34.080
right. This is needed. This is needed. I was very&nbsp;
fortunate when I was 15, 16 to have a few mentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:41.560
male mentors, figures who, by a hook or crook,&nbsp;
were able to help me correct course in a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:49.000
of ways. If I had not had that input, I think&nbsp;
things could have ended, and I do mean ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:52.560
in a very terminal way, very differently.
Soman Chainani: We should talk about another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:52.560 --> 00:48:54.424
person on my list, then.
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.&nbsp;

00:48:54.424 --> 00:49:01.960
Soman Chainani: He just moved to Austin. His name&nbsp;
is Mike Regula R-E-G-U-L-A. He is an ex-Navy SEAL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:11.840
and he is here starting a company called Course&nbsp;
of Action, which what I want to do for teenagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.840 --> 00:49:22.720
he's doing for men. And it's using basic Navy SEAL&nbsp;
concepts and training to basically get men to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:28.200
both individually and in groups, look at their&nbsp;
lives and make proactive change to become more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:36.080
connected to what they want. So basically it's&nbsp;
every man who feels like they haven't quite found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:39.960
their way back onto a path or they've lost their&nbsp;
tribe, or anything like that. He's just trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:45.080
to find a safe space for men to work on, whether&nbsp;
it's the psychological part, the nutrition part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:51.720
the fitness part, the emotional part, and&nbsp;
in groups. And I think it's just so needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.720 --> 00:49:55.520
especially in this world. So I just think&nbsp;
he's someone to look out for. And I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:02.520
website is OneCOA. OneCOA, for Course of Action.
Tim Ferriss: We'll find it and we'll link to it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:08.240
well. How did you find Mike or Course of Action?
Soman Chainani: He was introduced to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:16.160
through — this is the best story. The boy I was in&nbsp;
love with in high school was this kid named Noah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:20.960
who I was obsessed with. And of course&nbsp;
he's now married with children, everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.960 --> 00:50:26.160
like that. And everyone knew this story, except&nbsp;
him, and then one day someone finally told him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:31.480
"You do realize that someone spent most of his&nbsp;
high school career trying to get your attention?"&nbsp;

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:38.000
And he just was like, "What?" And so then we&nbsp;
connected, became friends, and he's like, "You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:43.480
have to meet Mike," because, I don't know what it&nbsp;
was. Mike was in New York and he just felt like we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.480 --> 00:50:46.160
would bond, and we met, and that was that.
Tim Ferriss: That was that.&nbsp;

00:50:46.160 --> 00:50:51.040
Soman Chainani: He's just a special person.&nbsp;
And I just feel like — we were talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:58.200
this. The same way I feel about resuscitating&nbsp;
the teenage mind and soul, he feels about men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.880 --> 00:51:04.427
because I think he's seen it all,&nbsp;
having been an ex-SEAL, for sure.&nbsp;

00:51:04.427 --> 00:51:08.680
Tim Ferriss: Check it out. He's right here, right&nbsp;
here in my hometown, Mike Regula. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.680 --> 00:51:12.700
actually not the first time I've heard his name.&nbsp;
This rings a bell and I can't place the context.&nbsp;

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:14.027
Soman Chainani: Oh, he's&nbsp;
friends with all your friends.&nbsp;

00:51:14.027 --> 00:51:22.120
Tim Ferriss: Oh, there we go. There you go. That's&nbsp;
probably why I've heard his name. All right, does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:29.800
anything catch your eye that might make for a good&nbsp;
next Scooby Snack, conversationally speaking? We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:34.747
could go to — you know what? Here, how about this?
Soman Chainani: Oh, that's a good one.&nbsp;

00:51:34.747 --> 00:51:38.464
Tim Ferriss: All right, intermittent pop&nbsp;
star. This is in the absurd category.&nbsp;

00:51:38.464 --> 00:51:41.440
Soman Chainani: That's in the absurd category&nbsp;
because I should not still be doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:47.480
but in 2017 I went to this book festival&nbsp;
for the first time in Charleston called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:53.320
YALLFest, that is like the biggest book&nbsp;
festival for teenagers in the country.&nbsp;

00:51:53.320 --> 00:51:54.800
Tim Ferriss: No kidding.
Soman Chainani: Yeah, about 20,000 kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:59.120
descend on this thing. Everybody comes there. It's&nbsp;
where new projects are — it's just a big festival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:05.120
that takes over Charleston. And to get invited,&nbsp;
it means you've officially made it. And all I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:09.680
ever wanted in my career was to get invited&nbsp;
because your publisher can't pitch you. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:12.344
like one of these Sun Valley companies —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. You must be invited.&nbsp;

00:52:12.344 --> 00:52:16.800
Soman Chainani: Must be invited. So I finally&nbsp;
get invited. I'm so excited and I go and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.800 --> 00:52:20.560
the best two days of my life. I'm having the&nbsp;
most amazing time. And I go to the final show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:27.000
called the SmackDown, which they charge like $12&nbsp;
to get into. It's 3,000 people in Charleston Music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:32.400
Hall. It's the most beautiful theater. And the&nbsp;
authors participate in this variety show. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:40.280
sit there and watch the worst show I've ever seen&nbsp;
in my life. It is an abomination to make 3,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.280 --> 00:52:45.720
people sit through that. I was — at the fact that&nbsp;
I had to participate in some little part of it.&nbsp;

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:52.160
And I remember having this thing, secretly&nbsp;
thinking, I'm going to take over this show and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:52.160 --> 00:52:59.040
turn it into my own private pop concert, and it's&nbsp;
going to be amazing, and it's going to be awesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:59.040 --> 00:53:03.920
and it's going to be like the greatest show on&nbsp;
Earth. I just remember having this thought. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.920 --> 00:53:09.680
was just like, "This is my fantasy and it's going&nbsp;
to happen." I grew up idolizing — you know this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.680 --> 00:53:13.680
everyone who knows me knows this, but I should&nbsp;
probably say it. I grew up a huge Madonna fan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:17.760
That was my pop star growing up. I'm obsessed&nbsp;
with Taylor Swift. I'm obsessed with anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:17.760 --> 00:53:24.800
who can translate whatever their talent is into&nbsp;
some kind of cool showmanship. And so cut to two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:29.080
years later, they were like, "No one wants to&nbsp;
host this show. Do you want to host it?" And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:33.200
was like — because I'd earned enough cred to&nbsp;
be like, "Okay." I said, "Sure, as long as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:37.160
can completely redo it 100 percent my way."
Tim Ferriss: How did they think to come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.160 --> 00:53:41.720
you? Had you planted the seed somehow? Were you&nbsp;
like, "Not a bad show. I could do better," on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:46.160
the way out with a tear in the eye?
Soman Chainani: I think I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:52.264
"This could be better."
Tim Ferriss: Love your feedback.&nbsp;

00:53:52.264 --> 00:53:53.944
Soman Chainani: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: "I have a few words."&nbsp;

00:53:53.944 --> 00:53:55.744
Soman Chainani: "This could be better."
Tim Ferriss: Got it. So they came to you?&nbsp;

00:53:55.744 --> 00:54:01.480
Soman Chainani: Came to me. And then I was like,&nbsp;
"We're going to do the variety show. It's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:01.480 --> 00:54:07.800
to be all new games and it's going to start with&nbsp;
an eight-song, six-costume-change dance number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:07.800 --> 00:54:12.680
dedicated to whatever the themes are in YA."&nbsp;
Almost like a version of Billy Crystal's old Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:17.680
thing, but even more extreme. And so I don't know&nbsp;
what made me do it, but what was interesting is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:21.680
while we started working on it, and we got a&nbsp;
choreographer, and I don't know how to dance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:25.760
and I started learning these dance steps.&nbsp;
It was that process of training for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:30.560
learning how to dance, learning how to do&nbsp;
choreography, that was so awesome because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:34.800
it got me out of my head. And I started to fall&nbsp;
in love with this process of designing a show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:39.320
designing things, designing themes. It was a new&nbsp;
way of telling a story from beginning to end.&nbsp;

00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:45.840
How do you entertain an audience of 3,000 people&nbsp;
for 10 minutes? I fell in love with it and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:52.320
the best time, but then I did it — I think I did&nbsp;
it the next year. I've done it three times. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.320 --> 00:54:57.200
what I've realized is if I do it every two years&nbsp;
— because it's so much work. It's so much money of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.200 --> 00:55:02.491
my own, it's so much work, so much time.
Tim Ferriss: Right. In the red column.&nbsp;

00:55:02.491 --> 00:55:06.560
Soman Chainani: 100 percent. Just throwing&nbsp;
money on fire. But if I do it every two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:12.600
that means the year in between someone else, some&nbsp;
poor soul has to come and try, and it's inevitably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.600 --> 00:55:18.584
a disaster. So then when I come back, I get —
Tim Ferriss: Herald! The hero returns.&nbsp;

00:55:18.584 --> 00:55:26.760
Soman Chainani: Every two years. Julia Roberts'&nbsp;
strategy was brilliant when she was an actress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:31.120
The reason she had such longevity is she&nbsp;
always did one movie where she had her hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:35.680
red and curly and did a romcom, and then she&nbsp;
would do one when she hacked it off and played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:42.720
like Frankenstein's maid. Some crazy, indie weird&nbsp;
thing. But because she did it every other movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:46.640
whenever she came back it was like, "Oh,&nbsp;
thank God. She's back to doing Pretty Woman."&nbsp;

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:52.240
And what's been important for me is, it's less&nbsp;
about — I get more than enough attention for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.240 --> 00:55:58.280
the books and everything. I don't need — it's&nbsp;
not a — sure, it is a narcissistic thing. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.280 --> 00:56:08.200
am I saying? This is such a lie. What a lie.&nbsp;
Terrible. But I think what I enjoy more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:15.000
the actual performance is the run-up. It's the day&nbsp;
before rehearsals start. I'm always nauseous. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:20.920
don't want to do it. I don't want to go through&nbsp;
that whole experience, because it's everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:28.320
I'm bad at. There's a Japanese word. Have you&nbsp;
heard Misogi? There's a book about this that —&nbsp;

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:31.360
Tim Ferriss: I'd have to look it up. Yeah.
Soman Chainani: To find happiness. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:31.360 --> 00:56:36.280
Mike was telling me about this. The Japanese&nbsp;
concept that once a year or once every two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:42.960
years you have to do something completely out of&nbsp;
your comfort zone that is an extended journey. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:49.320
in doing that, you find new reinvigoration for&nbsp;
life. And I think that's mine. Every two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:53.120
to go into this thing where I don't know how&nbsp;
it's going to end, I don't know if it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:58.160
work because always it's more ambitious and more&nbsp;
interesting. So it's funny because this one was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.160 --> 00:57:03.720
bigger and crazier, I just did it a couple months&nbsp;
ago, than anything that we'd ever done before.&nbsp;

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:08.840
And at the party I was like, "There's no&nbsp;
way. There's just — I know myself. I can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:12.840
top that scale, what we did." And someone&nbsp;
goes, "Well, what are you going to do now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:25.480
Are you going to sing?" And I was like,&nbsp;
"Haha." And then I thought, huh, well —&nbsp;

00:57:25.480 --> 00:57:26.440
Tim Ferriss: Ding.
Soman Chainani: I doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.440 --> 00:57:29.400
that's what it's going to be. But it's always&nbsp;
like, when you think you can't find what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:34.520
next thing is, it's like, what's the next zone of&nbsp;
discomfort? So that's the silly thing I still do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:41.585
but I think it's important for me somehow.&nbsp;
I can't really let go of it at this point.&nbsp;

00:57:41.585 --> 00:57:42.880
Tim Ferriss: It does something for you.
Soman Chainani: Yeah, definitely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.880 --> 00:57:44.480
Definitely. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Well, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:49.600
would your life be different if it were taken&nbsp;
away? If it were just subtracted? How would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:54.760
your experience the rest of the year be&nbsp;
different? If there's an answer to that.&nbsp;

00:57:54.760 --> 00:58:01.600
Soman Chainani: Yeah. It wouldn't be as&nbsp;
fun. There's something so energizing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.600 --> 00:58:08.840
the high-stakes nature of a show. And also my&nbsp;
assistant is this amazing dancer and musician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.840 --> 00:58:12.840
so he's part of it. I have backup&nbsp;
dancers and he's one of them. So, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:18.120
like he's — we're all on this ride together where&nbsp;
it can end in super embarrassment, because you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:18.120 --> 00:58:23.400
got thousands of people with their phones out.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. There's no, "You had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.400 --> 00:58:27.440
there." There's, "Let me show you what happened."
Soman Chainani: It feels very high stakes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:31.040
in the moment. So, where writing&nbsp;
is never in the moment really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:34.347
because you are constantly revising. This&nbsp;
is immediate, and I just think it's awesome.&nbsp;

00:58:34.347 --> 00:58:35.984
Tim Ferriss: It's also out of&nbsp;
your head, like you mentioned.&nbsp;

00:58:35.984 --> 00:58:39.160
Soman Chainani: Yes. Yeah. I always&nbsp;
feel in incredible shape mentally and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:44.240
physically after six weeks of this stuff. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to force a segue. We'll see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.240 --> 00:58:48.022
if it's a natural segue, but drugs.
Soman Chainani: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:58:48.022 --> 00:58:50.510
Tim Ferriss: Now, this is in the&nbsp;
yellow, I believe, change your mind —&nbsp;

00:58:50.510 --> 00:58:52.421
Soman Chainani: Change your mind.
Tim Ferriss: — about category.&nbsp;

00:58:52.421 --> 00:58:57.040
Soman Chainani: So I never did drugs growing up.&nbsp;
I maybe did pot once or twice and found it slowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.040 --> 00:59:03.360
me down to the point of I never wanted to do it&nbsp;
again. And just sort of walled that whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:10.960
off as, I don't know, stuff that other people do.&nbsp;
And then, during COVID, I read The Body Keeps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:19.960
Score. And I had struggled with some things for my&nbsp;
entire life in terms of anxiety and just getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.960 --> 00:59:27.080
stuck in kind of ruts of feeling. And it was&nbsp;
2021 January where I just remember, talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:27.080 --> 00:59:33.520
follow the flow, I stood up, went to my computer.&nbsp;
I remember being in Miami at my parents' house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:33.520 --> 00:59:41.187
Googling "Ketamine treatment, New York City."&nbsp;
I knew nothing about ketamine, nothing.&nbsp;

00:59:41.187 --> 00:59:42.864
Tim Ferriss: How was it even in your field?
Soman Chainani: I don't know.&nbsp;

00:59:42.864 --> 00:59:44.720
Tim Ferriss: It just popped into the head.
Soman Chainani: I don't know. I just remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:51.160
going "Ketamine therapy in New York City," calling&nbsp;
up, scheduling the consultation. And a week later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.160 --> 00:59:55.200
I'm back in New York going to meet the&nbsp;
doctor. And I was like, "I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.200 --> 00:59:59.320
if I'm right for this. I don't want anything&nbsp;
that will mess with my creativity." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.320 --> 01:00:04.800
"The only reason I'm entertaining this idea&nbsp;
is because it's in a doctor's office." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.800 --> 01:00:12.040
"I would never do drugs on my own because the&nbsp;
fear surrounding that of what could happen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:19.280
where it's procured from and all that stuff would&nbsp;
end up infecting the whole experience. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:22.600
a doctor's office, I'm entertaining it."
I said, "But I don't want it to affect my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:22.600 --> 01:00:26.360
creativity. I don't want it to..." He's like,&nbsp;
"It's only going to make you more yourself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:26.360 --> 01:00:34.520
more creative." But he goes, "These are the three&nbsp;
things that I think make for a good candidate."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:34.520 --> 01:00:40.440
He's like, "Number one, have you felt emotionally&nbsp;
numb for most of your life?" And at that point in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:46.840
my life, absolutely. I think I was in just&nbsp;
cycles of numbness. Number two was, "Did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.840 --> 01:00:55.507
have a volatile childhood where emotions were not&nbsp;
particularly welcome?" And I was like, "Check."&nbsp;

01:00:55.507 --> 01:00:58.360
Tim Ferriss: Check.
Soman Chainani: Check. And number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.360 --> 01:01:05.520
he goes, "This one's the most important." He's&nbsp;
like, "Do you know how to have fun?" And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:10.360
"No." And I cried. I remember crying in the&nbsp;
office. He's like, "Do you know how to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.360 --> 01:01:15.920
fun?" And it was the first time anyone had asked&nbsp;
the question in the right way. Because I knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.920 --> 01:01:21.000
idea of fun. I knew that I should be having&nbsp;
fun. I knew how to act like I was having fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:27.160
but I never felt like I was having fun. And he&nbsp;
goes, "You don't drink?" I said, "No." And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.160 --> 01:01:33.360
goes — and I meditate. I was that point, four or&nbsp;
five years into meditation. He's like, "You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:37.720
going to have a very good response to this."&nbsp;
He just instantly — and so, I started ketamine&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:37.720 --> 01:01:43.480
treatments. At that time, I think you do six&nbsp;
in 12 days, and then you go back for a booster.&nbsp;

01:01:43.480 --> 01:01:49.320
In my case, it's every 10 weeks and I still go.&nbsp;
And it changed my life. I mean, it was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:49.320 --> 01:01:54.000
I feel like a totally different person because of&nbsp;
those. Because what it did is it woke up parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:59.840
my brain that I did know existed because they&nbsp;
had shut off so early that I didn't know they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.840 --> 01:02:07.120
were there. And then, all of a sudden I started&nbsp;
to get glimpses of what I could be like. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.120 --> 01:02:10.560
it became a question of could I hold&nbsp;
onto that? And little by little over —&nbsp;

01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:14.320
Some people, I think, get their benefits much&nbsp;
quicker, or they don't get any benefits at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:14.320 --> 01:02:18.600
all. I mean, it's so individual. In my case,&nbsp;
it was almost like a practice. It was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:18.600 --> 01:02:24.680
every 10 weeks, I'd go learn a million&nbsp;
things, work on those things in between,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.680 --> 01:02:36.800
go back. And it almost became my version of the&nbsp;
deepest therapy. And I think it's become a huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:43.520
just, reason for everything. And I think it's&nbsp;
also allowed me to follow the flow more than ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:43.520 --> 01:02:48.520
because the whole thing of a ketamine&nbsp;
treatment is it's a sedative. Right? So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.520 --> 01:02:51.760
it puts your brain in such a relaxed state that&nbsp;
you have no choice but to follow the flow where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.760 --> 01:02:57.800
it goes. And the flow usually leads you closer to&nbsp;
the truth. So, I don't know. I think it's — for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.800 --> 01:03:03.837
someone who was so anti-drugs, it was the biggest&nbsp;
thing that changed my mind about them. Yeah.&nbsp;

01:03:03.837 --> 01:03:08.400
Tim Ferriss: In those — well, let's see. Just to&nbsp;
establish some basics, with the whatever it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:15.080
let's call it six sessions in 12 days. So, that&nbsp;
loading phase, let's call it, induction phase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:15.080 --> 01:03:18.408
those were IV? Or —
Soman Chainani: IV.&nbsp;

01:03:18.408 --> 01:03:19.200
Tim Ferriss: IV.
Soman Chainani: I've never done&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:24.760
the micro-dosing and the nasal spray and all that&nbsp;
