01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:57.600
or just use, extensive use, which&nbsp;
certainly if you go to New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:57.600 --> 01:04:03.553
and I've spent a lot of time in New York City,&nbsp;
a lot of social life revolves around alcohol.&nbsp;

01:04:03.553 --> 01:04:04.480
Soman Chainani: 100 percent. Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: And many people there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.480 --> 01:04:08.960
have switched to ketamine, but it's still a&nbsp;
dissociative anesthetic. So, in those uncontrolled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:17.600
environments, people can find themselves in a&nbsp;
slide that can become very precarious. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:17.600 --> 01:04:24.440
the clinical setting, I also find ketamine to be&nbsp;
incredibly interesting. There's a lot of research&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.440 --> 01:04:29.000
people can find on PubMed and elsewhere looking&nbsp;
at the rapid antidepressant effects ketamine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:34.200
their different types. For people who might be&nbsp;
interested in the potential benefits and risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:34.200 --> 01:04:40.200
and science related to this, I did an episode&nbsp;
with Dr. John Krystal of Yale who did — he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:46.080
the primary investigator on a lot of the seminal&nbsp;
work investigating ketamine as antidepressant in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:46.080 --> 01:04:52.264
humans and developed some of the early protocols&nbsp;
with his fellow researchers and scientists.&nbsp;

01:04:52.264 --> 01:04:57.440
Soman Chainani: Which is tricky because anytime&nbsp;
anybody, I talk to people close to me, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:57.440 --> 01:05:02.000
like, "Do you recommend it?" I'm like, "No." And&nbsp;
I never recommend it to people. I don't think I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:09.280
ever recommended it to anyone because I found&nbsp;
it difficult. It's a journey into the deepest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.280 --> 01:05:15.000
core of yourself. It's a lot. It's a lot of work.
Tim Ferriss: And the meditation background also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:15.000 --> 01:05:20.424
serves you, and I would imagine, in a very big&nbsp;
way in terms of navigating some of these recesses.&nbsp;

01:05:20.424 --> 01:05:22.600
Soman Chainani: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: What do your boosters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.600 --> 01:05:25.587
