I have not felt in probably a month."
Soman Chainani: It's amazing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:56.360
anything that's coming from a place of stress —&nbsp;
but the thing is, it could return if you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:56.360 --> 01:07:58.960
deal with that, if you don't deal with that&nbsp;
psychological stress, it might return, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.960 --> 01:08:03.000
yeah. Yeah. Yeah. For sure. I felt that too. If&nbsp;
I go in with a lot of muscle soreness, I'll come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:05.920
back out and it's almost like it never happened.
Tim Ferriss: So, for people who want to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:05.920 --> 01:08:12.040
more, certainly John Krystal, with a K, Krystal,&nbsp;
and Andrew Huberman also recently had an episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.040 --> 01:08:13.863
related to all things ketamine —
Soman Chainani: Oh, interesting.&nbsp;

01:08:13.863 --> 01:08:16.120
Tim Ferriss: — which I'm sure&nbsp;
involved a lot of due diligence. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:16.120 --> 01:08:20.744
you can check that out. "The double." This is in&nbsp;
the excited about category. What is the double?&nbsp;

01:08:20.744 --> 01:08:24.160
Soman Chainani: Super excited about.&nbsp;
I've been thinking about this one a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.160 --> 01:08:31.560
which is there's so much work on your shadow&nbsp;
self in psychology. It's always like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:38.440
get control of your shadow self. Your shadow self&nbsp;
is..." And the more I think about it, and the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:38.440 --> 01:08:45.520
I've gone through my journeys and all my kind of&nbsp;
evolutions, I'm realizing that most of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.520 --> 01:08:49.504
you are the shadow self. You. It's you —
Tim Ferriss: The primary actor.&nbsp;

01:08:49.504 --> 01:08:54.800
Soman Chainani: — you are the problem. The better&nbsp;
version of yourself is actually out there. Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.800 --> 01:08:59.800
double. Right? And so, I think you have to flip&nbsp;
it, and at any given moment, and it's a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.800 --> 01:09:06.880
exercise to do all day long, just a mental thought&nbsp;
experiment, of what if I'm the shadow and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.880 --> 01:09:14.880
a better version of myself, completely independent&nbsp;
human outside that door that could take over this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.880 --> 01:09:21.080
life and completely handle it with no issues.
And so, it's been something that, if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:21.080 --> 01:09:26.640
at all my fiction, all my fiction is about&nbsp;
some version of twins, one good, one evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:32.360
one this, one that. And it's always — I think it's&nbsp;
that. It's that I'm always trying to figure out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:32.360 --> 01:09:38.040
am I who I'm supposed to be? And I&nbsp;
think often psychology will say, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.040 --> 01:09:41.520
you have to get control of that shadow."&nbsp;
And sometimes I'm like, "Well, no. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:48.840
I just need to not be here and let the double&nbsp;
be in charge." And so, I think it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.840 --> 01:09:53.880
I'll be exploring in everything I do, because I&nbsp;
think it's such an important concept that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:53.880 --> 01:09:58.680
reversed it and that we shouldn't be afraid of&nbsp;
the shadow, because the shadow is actually —&nbsp;

01:09:58.680 --> 01:10:01.544
Tim Ferriss: Staring right back at us.
Soman Chainani: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:10:01.544 --> 01:10:10.320
Tim Ferriss: So when does this thought, this&nbsp;
concept, come up for you? I have a friend who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.320 --> 01:10:15.400
calm in almost all circumstances, certainly&nbsp;
compared to me, Matt Mullenweg. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.400 --> 01:10:22.120
have been times when I have thought to myself,&nbsp;
"What would Matt Mullenweg do?" Almost like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:22.120 --> 01:10:27.480
reminder bracelet that some people have for&nbsp;
JC and so on. And that has been very helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:27.480 --> 01:10:33.560
When I'm in a situation, I feel myself on the&nbsp;
verge of becoming dysregulated. Not like "Hulk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:33.560 --> 01:10:41.440
smash," but just having some type of rumination or&nbsp;
tantrum internally that is not helpful. I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.440 --> 01:10:45.960
"Pause. What would Matt Mullenweg do?" And I'm&nbsp;
like, "You know how he would handle this is he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:49.360
would do da da da." And then, sometimes I just&nbsp;
do that thing and I'm like, "Well, that wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.360 --> 01:10:56.280
so hard." So I'm curious if there are times&nbsp;
when you feel your lesser self doing something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:56.280 --> 01:10:59.800
or I guess in this case, your shadow,&nbsp;
right, doing something where you're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:59.800 --> 01:11:04.320
"What would my double do?" How does it come up?
Soman Chainani: It came up first because I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.320 --> 01:11:10.800
going to be on The Kelly Clarkson Show two years&nbsp;
ago, and I had never been on kind of a TV show&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.800 --> 01:11:15.960
like that. And it was live studio audience.&nbsp;
It was straight out of COVID. And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:19.920
"I'm just — I'm not ready for it.&nbsp;
I just don't know how to do this."&nbsp;

01:11:19.920 --> 01:11:24.464
Tim Ferriss: Did you decide to do&nbsp;
that or that came suggested to you?&nbsp;

01:11:24.464 --> 01:11:27.920
Soman Chainani: Yeah. Yeah. So, the publicist was&nbsp;
like, "We booked you on Kelly." And I was like, "I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.920 --> 01:11:33.640
don't know." Especially after lockdown, and I was&nbsp;
just not ready for, I think one of the first shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.640 --> 01:11:40.240
that she had people back. And I remember in the&nbsp;
dressing rooms starting to freak out and panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:40.240 --> 01:11:47.240
and I could feel that panic rising. And then, all&nbsp;
of a sudden, I felt this counterforce where I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:47.240 --> 01:11:53.560
stressing on my phone. I felt myself put the phone&nbsp;
down and I just felt some other presence be like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.800 --> 01:12:01.280
"It's fine. We've got this." And I almost remember&nbsp;
in that moment being like, "Okay." I just felt it.&nbsp;

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:05.640
And that was the first time I'd ever felt&nbsp;
something like that. Because I remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:05.640 --> 01:12:11.200
sitting on the couch and feeling totally chill.&nbsp;
David Duchovny was next to me. I had grown up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.200 --> 01:12:18.200
watching X-Files. I made a sex joke with him in&nbsp;
the first two seconds of the thing. I was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:23.040
totally chill. And I just was like, "What?"&nbsp;
I'm literally watching myself being like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.040 --> 01:12:28.747
"What is happening?" And that was the first&nbsp;
time I got a sense of like, "Oh, wait…"&nbsp;

01:12:28.747 --> 01:12:30.880
Tim Ferriss: The possibility.
Soman Chainani: That there's a better version that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.880 --> 01:12:35.760
can handle every situation. And I remember the&nbsp;
other thing that gave me a clue was Tiger Woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:41.440
There was a documentary about Tiger Woods on HBO,&nbsp;
and they talked about how his dad taught him kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.440 --> 01:12:48.720
of techniques to almost hypnotize himself when he&nbsp;
played so that his conscious mind wasn't working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:48.720 --> 01:12:54.320
and that's how he dominated all those years. And&nbsp;
I thought, that's it. It's almost like you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.320 --> 01:12:58.200
get your conscious brain out of the way because&nbsp;
the double, the real version of yourself that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.200 --> 01:13:04.360
knows how to do everything, is there somewhere.&nbsp;
And the question is, over time, can you make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.360 --> 01:13:08.880
so they start to integrate? And then, a useful&nbsp;
thing that also helped me start to explore it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

