undeniable. It will exist forever because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:39.560 --> 01:27:46.480
that committed. And so, those two authors, I think&nbsp;
are a sign that no matter how addled, or stupid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.480 --> 01:27:54.720
or short-term thinking, or monkey meme, we get,&nbsp;
it doesn't matter. That stuff still holds because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:54.720 --> 01:27:59.440
they're the two most popular authors on TikTok.
Tim Ferriss: Why do you think they are? And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:59.440 --> 01:28:07.640
know that you just explained part of the picture&nbsp;
in your mind, why they are so popular on TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:07.640 --> 01:28:13.640
because there are many dedicated authors,&nbsp;
let's just take as a category of profession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:13.640 --> 01:28:21.800
who release books infrequently, who are supremely&nbsp;
good at the craft, who are not popular on TikTok.&nbsp;

01:28:22.800 --> 01:28:27.640
So what do you think it is about these two, even&nbsp;
if you had to just make stuff up, speculating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:27.640 --> 01:28:31.784
what do you think it is about these two&nbsp;
in particular? What else adds to that?&nbsp;

01:28:31.784 --> 01:28:33.861
Soman Chainani: It goes with&nbsp;
this one. So, this is together.&nbsp;

01:28:33.861 --> 01:28:36.960
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Levels of feeling.
Soman Chainani: Which is together with Sally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:36.960 --> 01:28:43.400
Rooney, who's another author who I'm obsessed&nbsp;
with, these three authors are able to access a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:43.400 --> 01:28:49.120
certain kind of primal nerve of feeling because&nbsp;
they're so immersed in it, they're just there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:49.120 --> 01:28:55.720
that it's the intensity of reading it just&nbsp;
feels higher than anything else. Like yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.720 --> 01:28:59.880
you can have someone who spent 10 years crafting&nbsp;
this meticulous world and all that sort of stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:59.880 --> 01:29:06.400
I've read plenty of books like that, but there's&nbsp;
something about the 10 years that is spent doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.400 --> 01:29:10.600
that work we talked about, what does the character&nbsp;
want? Why do they want it? Why do they want that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.600 --> 01:29:14.080
Why do they want that? And they do it for every&nbsp;
character so that when you're reading it, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:14.080 --> 01:29:20.480
just in this emotional soup that is undeniable.&nbsp;
It's just so affecting to everybody who reads it.&nbsp;

01:29:20.480 --> 01:29:26.600
So there's this category of book that I can&nbsp;
recommend to anybody who comes to me and says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:26.600 --> 01:29:31.600
"What book should I read?" I always tell them&nbsp;
Secret History by Donna Tartt, A Little Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:31.600 --> 01:29:36.080
by Hanya, Conversations with Friends&nbsp;
by Sally Rooney. Those are my first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:36.080 --> 01:29:43.160
three recommendations. And it doesn't matter,&nbsp;
man, woman, young, old, it always works out.&nbsp;

01:29:43.160 --> 01:29:47.360
Tim Ferriss: I have not read any of those three.
Soman Chainani: Well, which one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:47.360 --> 01:29:48.800
should you start with?
Tim Ferriss: That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.800 --> 01:29:53.600
question, which one should I start with and why?
Soman Chainani: Oh, boy, they're all so different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.600 --> 01:29:55.720
It depends on their mood. A Little Life —
Tim Ferriss: Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.720 --> 01:30:02.400
you can interrogate my mood then.
Soman Chainani: A Little Life is if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:02.400 --> 01:30:06.424
in the mood to be kind of torn apart.
Tim Ferriss: Not right now.&nbsp;

01:30:06.424 --> 01:30:11.267
Soman Chainani: Yeah. So then I would wait for&nbsp;
that one. That one, it's almost like a hijack.&nbsp;

01:30:11.267 --> 01:30:12.360
Tim Ferriss: I'm open to that.
Soman Chainani: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:12.360 --> 01:30:17.424
it's an emotional hijack. You will disappear.
Tim Ferriss: I'm open to that, just not right now.&nbsp;

01:30:17.424 --> 01:30:24.920
Soman Chainani: Conversations with Friends is the&nbsp;
most accurate depiction of what it's like to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:24.920 --> 01:30:30.200
I don't know this for sure, but I just mean&nbsp;
what I think of what it means to sort of be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.200 --> 01:30:40.800
in a feminine state of mind in your 20s. It is&nbsp;
just crystal clear and brilliant writing of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:40.800 --> 01:30:46.920
emotions of what it's like to be young. And so&nbsp;
I just think for anyone who has ever been young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:46.920 --> 01:30:52.120
when you read it, it takes you back there. It&nbsp;
takes over everything. So I think that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:52.120 --> 01:30:56.720
and you'll tear through it in a day and&nbsp;
a half because you won't be able to stop.&nbsp;

01:30:56.720 --> 01:31:03.280
Secret History is if you just want great writing&nbsp;
and feel like you're in a different world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:03.280 --> 01:31:08.840
because Donna takes her 10 years to meticulously&nbsp;
create a world that the characters feel so real&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:08.840 --> 01:31:13.880
that you're there. So if you're just ready to&nbsp;
go somewhere else and disappear for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:13.880 --> 01:31:20.320
but without the emotional hijacking, you go to&nbsp;
Donna Tartt. If you want to be emotionally thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:20.320 --> 01:31:27.680
into a well, Hanya, and if you just want the&nbsp;
equivalent of a Taylor Swift album in prose that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:27.680 --> 01:31:35.120
is much more articulate, you go to Sally Rooney.
Tim Ferriss: Conversations with Friends. Perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.120 --> 01:31:39.760
all right. Where should we go next? Now, I'm&nbsp;
enjoying this game because as we flip these over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:39.760 --> 01:31:45.147
we get to fewer and fewer options.
Soman Chainani: These three —&nbsp;

01:31:45.147 --> 01:31:47.387
Tim Ferriss: Interrelated?
Soman Chainani: Are going to all go together.&nbsp;

01:31:47.387 --> 01:31:53.840
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so we've got two yellows,&nbsp;
which are changed mind about, "Cross-collar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:53.840 --> 01:32:00.184
dating," not sure what that is, "Hookups,"&nbsp;
suspect I know what that is, "Couples language."&nbsp;

01:32:00.184 --> 01:32:03.160
Soman Chainani: Maybe we should start with&nbsp;
cross-collar dating then, because that's sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:03.160 --> 01:32:07.640
the unlocking key. Have you never heard this term?
Tim Ferriss: I've never heard this term. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:07.640 --> 01:32:13.960
what a cross-collar throw is in judo, but I'm&nbsp;
guessing this is unrelated. Cross-collar dating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:13.960 --> 01:32:16.507
Is that like white collar, blue collar?
Soman Chainani: Yes.&nbsp;

01:32:16.507 --> 01:32:18.000
Tim Ferriss: I'm just guessing.
Soman Chainani: Yes, yes, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:18.000 --> 01:32:23.840
it. And it's been popular now, I've just seen&nbsp;
there are a lot of columns about this and things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:23.840 --> 01:32:27.960
like that, which is also why when I thought when&nbsp;
Taylor started dating Travis, I'm like, "They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:27.960 --> 01:32:36.120
going to last forever," because they just will.&nbsp;
Now watch, I'll have to eat my words. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:36.120 --> 01:32:40.680
similar to what happened to me, which was I lived&nbsp;
in New York for 22 years. I dated every lawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:40.680 --> 01:32:48.880
accountant, investment banker out there, I went&nbsp;
through it all and it always felt like two clouds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.880 --> 01:32:55.600
in the sky and no one on Earth — we were doing&nbsp;
jobs that a monkey would not understand. Could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.600 --> 01:33:01.440
you explain your job to a monkey? Neither of us,&nbsp;
like, "Oh, I write..." It wasn't going to work.&nbsp;

01:33:05.240 --> 01:33:11.680
So then when I met [redacted], who literally is&nbsp;
just in the Earth, he can tell you when it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:11.680 --> 01:33:19.960
to rain. It felt like it was just such a match&nbsp;
of someone who has Earth energy and someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:19.960 --> 01:33:24.880
has sky energy. Because in Hinduism, that's the&nbsp;
balance. It's not masculine/feminine, it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:24.880 --> 01:33:29.200
male/female, it's sky energy and Earth energy.
Tim Ferriss: I'm ashamed and astonished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:29.200 --> 01:33:31.920
I've never heard this before.
Soman Chainani: So that's the balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:31.920 --> 01:33:38.160
you're looking for. And I'm all sky, I'm all what&nbsp;
they call Shakti, which is thunder and lightning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:38.160 --> 01:33:44.800
that's me. So I need somebody who's the guy on&nbsp;
the ground holding the kite before it flies on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.880 --> 01:33:51.320
And so I just think of, what was I doing? I&nbsp;
should have known from the start. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.320 --> 01:33:56.160
I started to sense it this time around that I&nbsp;
needed to be with someone ultra grounded. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.160 --> 01:34:02.640
so that's why I think cross-collar dating works&nbsp;
because also you're not in each other's space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:02.640 --> 01:34:08.040
there's no competition. You're not competing&nbsp;
for the same thing. You're not measuring each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:08.040 --> 01:34:09.280
other against each other.
Tim Ferriss: Maybe you also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:09.280 --> 01:34:11.307
have different neuroses.
Soman Chainani: That's it.&nbsp;

01:34:11.307 --> 01:34:15.280
Tim Ferriss: Which is probably good.
Soman Chainani: Yeah, he's thinking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:15.280 --> 01:34:20.000
every one of [redacted] neuroses is about, does he&nbsp;
have enough money to sell this group of calves for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:20.000 --> 01:34:27.840
that? And I'm like, "I'm sure it's fine." That's&nbsp;
my reaction to everything, "I'm sure it's fine,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:27.840 --> 01:34:32.600
but I can't relate other than be supportive.&nbsp;
And when he comes to watch me do the dance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:32.600 --> 01:34:40.080
Charleston, he's just like, "This is a lot."
Tim Ferriss: Not sure what this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:40.080 --> 01:34:44.160
but you do you.
Soman Chainani: 100 percent. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.160 --> 01:34:49.360
calls it the dance of the red shoes, as a metaphor&nbsp;
in general. He's like, "He's off doing his dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:49.360 --> 01:34:53.640
of the red shoes. We'll see him when he's done,"&nbsp;
and that could be anything. So I think that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:53.640 --> 01:35:01.440
it is. And whenever I see two lawyers together,&nbsp;
I'm like, "Oh, no, who's going to...?" I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:01.440 --> 01:35:07.120
know. So yeah, something worth looking at.
Tim Ferriss: All right. Today I learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.120 --> 01:35:12.400
cross-collar dating. And now well actually,&nbsp;
look, I'll ask a self-serving question so I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:12.400 --> 01:35:19.720
back on the singles field, as you know, we've&nbsp;
talked about this privately. So what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.720 --> 01:35:24.960
that potentially mean for me? Is it more a set&nbsp;
of characteristics? Is it profession? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:24.960 --> 01:35:32.320
I have definitely, this is not exactly the same,&nbsp;
but I have had enough perhaps tiptoeing around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.320 --> 01:35:36.600
this to realize probably not a good idea for me&nbsp;
to be with another writer, you know what I mean?&nbsp;

01:35:36.600 --> 01:35:38.080
Soman Chainani: Agreed.
Tim Ferriss: I've taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.080 --> 01:35:46.200
that parking spot. I think that one is enough.
Soman Chainani: Agreed. I think it's as simple as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.200 --> 01:35:52.840
it's not so much the caller, it's about the&nbsp;
intensity of the commitment to what it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.840 --> 01:35:59.360
So for instance, I'm so committed to my writing&nbsp;
career and the entrepreneurship of my life that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:59.360 --> 01:36:06.200
is such a huge part of what I do that you want to&nbsp;
find somebody who has that same commitment to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:06.200 --> 01:36:12.400
they do that is in a completely different space.&nbsp;
So that's why I look at Taylor and Travis Kelce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:12.400 --> 01:36:19.120
because she's the most famous pop star in the&nbsp;
world, she's always in the number one position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.120 --> 01:36:26.440
What man is ever going to be able to have that&nbsp;
same level intensity but a football player playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:26.440 --> 01:36:31.027
in the same stadium in front of the same amount of&nbsp;
people, and she has to be there rooting for them.&nbsp;

01:36:31.027 --> 01:36:32.640
Tim Ferriss: It's perfect.
Soman Chainani: It's like you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:32.640 --> 01:36:38.120
each doing your thing, it's yours. It's not the&nbsp;
other person's, but the other person is there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.120 --> 01:36:43.920
cheerlead. And I think that to me is the perfect&nbsp;
recipe of where — and I think sometimes when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:43.920 --> 01:36:46.840
have, even if it's an investment banker and&nbsp;
a lawyer, they're similar, there's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:46.840 --> 01:36:51.640
similar about it. But if you can find something&nbsp;
that's really different so that you can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:51.640 --> 01:36:57.400
relax and be a cheerleader and feel almost humble,&nbsp;
like [redacted] coaches basketball and when I go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:57.400 --> 01:37:01.920
watch the games, it's just fun to watch him coach&nbsp;
basketball because that has nothing to do with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:01.920 --> 01:37:03.840
existence. I have nothing to do with it.
Tim Ferriss: I didn't know that part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:03.840 --> 01:37:06.440
you must love that. I'm just&nbsp;
thinking of you with the tutoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:06.440 --> 01:37:10.360
and also tennis background. You must love it.
Soman Chainani: And he comes to me with all his,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:10.920 --> 01:37:14.720
"This kid this. What do I say to this kid,&nbsp;
this?" And I'm just always asking him questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:14.720 --> 01:37:19.360
I love it. It's my favorite thing. I'm always&nbsp;
asking him more questions about what's happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:19.360 --> 01:37:22.680
with the drama behind the scenes.
Tim Ferriss: I'm still thinking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:22.680 --> 01:37:26.800
the dance of the red shoes. I may use&nbsp;
that with you, that's great. All right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:26.800 --> 01:37:30.760
where should we go? Hookups or couples language?
Soman Chainani: Well, couples language is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.760 --> 01:37:34.903
quick one, but I think it's really interesting&nbsp;
that with couples, I feel like every couple —&nbsp;

01:37:34.903 --> 01:37:37.864
Tim Ferriss: Oh, this is in the&nbsp;
absurd category, just so people know.&nbsp;

01:37:37.864 --> 01:37:42.000
Soman Chainani: Because I thought it was just&nbsp;
us, we have our own language at times, words we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:42.000 --> 01:37:48.400
make up and things that we have that no one else&nbsp;
would understand. And it's almost like animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:48.400 --> 01:37:54.360
we just came up with our own little barks&nbsp;
and things and repeated phrases and repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:54.360 --> 01:38:00.120
thoughts that are just ours. And then I talked&nbsp;
to other couples and they have it too. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:00.120 --> 01:38:04.800
was talking to a friend about this where he's&nbsp;
like, him and his partner used to, instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:04.800 --> 01:38:10.200
saying the word "program," they used to use&nbsp;
a British accent for it and say "program" or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:10.200 --> 01:38:17.600
something. And that was how they always said it.&nbsp;
And then they broke up and then the new partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:17.600 --> 01:38:25.787
heard about this somehow and started using it&nbsp;
and he felt this irrational sense of anger.&nbsp;

01:38:25.787 --> 01:38:28.560
Tim Ferriss: Like aversion to it, yeah.
Soman Chainani: Yeah, because it's somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:28.560 --> 01:38:34.920
else's language. That's when I thought, "Oh, no,&nbsp;
this is a real thing. This is somehow in our DNA&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:34.920 --> 01:38:40.640
to create this private language with our other&nbsp;
person." [redacted] and I have a million of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:44.624
but we use the word "malinky" a lot.
Tim Ferriss: Malinkey?&nbsp;

01:38:44.624 --> 01:38:50.080
Soman Chainani: Malinkey to refer to something&nbsp;
that is on its way to being totally fucked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:50.080 --> 01:38:56.920
but has not gotten there. It's circling the drain.
Tim Ferriss: Is this an adjective or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:56.920 --> 01:39:00.000
noun? It is a malinky or it is —
Soman Chainani: No, it is malinky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:05.640
it's an adjective. So we use that a lot. A&nbsp;
person could be malinky, a dish that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:05.640 --> 01:39:10.640
trying to cook could be malinky. So it just&nbsp;
comes up. And so we have maybe a hundred of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:10.640 --> 01:39:15.627
these kind of words. And so nicknames and things,&nbsp;
and so it's just our own little private language.&nbsp;

01:39:15.627 --> 01:39:19.800
Tim Ferriss: Hookups.
Soman Chainani: This is a pretty simple one, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:19.800 --> 01:39:27.160
in New York, hookup culture was big and I never&nbsp;
really got into it. I always was more interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.160 --> 01:39:33.200
in connections and things like that. And people&nbsp;
are like, "But it's fun, so many hot guys," I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:33.200 --> 01:39:39.120
mean, New York, Austin is similar, everywhere you&nbsp;
go, everyone's like a supermodel, it's annoying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:39.120 --> 01:39:46.240
But the reason that it stopped me was every time&nbsp;
I was like, "Okay, you could go hook up with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.240 --> 01:39:52.920
guy, whatever." I used to think to myself, "Well,&nbsp;
what's the reason?" And I realized that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:52.920 --> 01:39:57.360
because I was trying to extract something from&nbsp;
them. They had this hotness or this thing or this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:57.360 --> 01:40:03.640
thing that I felt that by hooking up with them,&nbsp;
I would get, right? And that's when I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:03.640 --> 01:40:11.080
thinking about the term hookup. What is it?&nbsp;
Hook? It's like you're trying to literally like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:11.080 --> 01:40:17.800
a fish hook them and get this thing. There's no&nbsp;
connection needed, you're going to dispose of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:17.800 --> 01:40:21.000
at some point, you know it's not going to last.
Tim Ferriss: You're just going to lean over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:21.000 --> 01:40:22.440
breathe in their soul.
Soman Chainani: That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:22.440 --> 01:40:24.504
it. And I thought to myself —
Tim Ferriss: Sell their chi.&nbsp;

01:40:24.504 --> 01:40:28.800
Soman Chainani: And in my case, it was obvious&nbsp;
because it felt like I was going after certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.800 --> 01:40:33.760
kind of guys that had things that I wanted. And I&nbsp;
realized that it was never because I liked them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:33.760 --> 01:40:37.880
it was because I wanted to be them or I wanted&nbsp;
this thing that they had physically or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.880 --> 01:40:44.640
And it was always like, you just want to extract.&nbsp;
And I thought, "That can't be good for your soul."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:44.640 --> 01:40:48.040
I just thought that can't be good because what's&nbsp;
going to happen if you mentally go through with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:48.040 --> 01:40:51.320
it? Yes, you're going to go, you're going to&nbsp;
extract this thing or try to extract it, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:51.320 --> 01:40:54.427
going to fail to extract it and they're going&nbsp;
to leave and you're still not going to have it.&nbsp;

01:40:54.427 --> 01:41:00.480
Tim Ferriss: I think New York, as you&nbsp;
described it, is just such a hyperkinetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:00.480 --> 01:41:07.080
environment that I feel like whether it's&nbsp;
related to hookups or alcohol or other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:07.080 --> 01:41:13.064
you need to have some guardrails or I feel like&nbsp;
that environment will sort of eat you alive.&nbsp;

01:41:13.064 --> 01:41:17.520
Soman Chainani: I think it's funny because&nbsp;
we can move to that one now that we're here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:17.520 --> 01:41:25.400
I lived there for 22 years and I would go to&nbsp;
Florida and go to places that were much slower&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.400 --> 01:41:31.040
to relax. But I think with New York and L.A.&nbsp;
what I realized is until you're out of there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:31.040 --> 01:41:37.320
for an extended period of time, that's when&nbsp;
you finally get to be yourself. Because New&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:37.320 --> 01:41:43.480
York and L.A. and even Chicago and the biggest&nbsp;
city, San Francisco, everything's so reactive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:44.000 --> 01:41:50.200
It's too much stimulus to ever process in a way&nbsp;
where you're just alone with your thoughts in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.200 --> 01:41:56.680
consistent way. So it's not just vacation, but on&nbsp;
a permanent basis. And when I moved to St. Louis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.680 --> 01:42:02.360
I didn't have any friends there, I was new to the&nbsp;
city, I knew no one, still getting used to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.360 --> 01:42:09.680
but it's quiet, not much is happening. And for&nbsp;
the first time in my life, it was just me. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.680 --> 01:42:15.320
I felt like in those 10 months I had to grow.
Tim Ferriss: I got it. So you had a 10-month&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.320 --> 01:42:18.067
period where you were there full time?
